86483284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483284 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483284 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483284 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483284 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186482839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483284 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "70d3adf1-3aa8-506a-a4e2-98cf256eb5b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483353 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483353 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483353 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483353 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=10x-2\\sqrt{15}y\\,$ on the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483353 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27a9cc77-ffaa-5079-b6d3-f595b1244e8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483353 #edb/ref 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨10,-2\\sqrt{15}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨10x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}10=10\\lambda x \\\\ -2\\sqrt{15}=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=5$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{15}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5+15}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-20\\,$ | $\\,20\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-20.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483364 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4x-2y+1\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4126f1c5-7567-585d-9e49-d37c72e8e18c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483364 #edb/ref 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=5$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2,1),\\,(2,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(2,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-9$ | $11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2,1),$ and its minimum value is $-9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483396 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483396 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483396 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483396 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,$ on the parabola ${x}^{2}-2y-4=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483396 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0386d5f-8012-5e50-aca9-5585df04ede1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483396 #edb/ref 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-2y-4=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=-2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-2y-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,-2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-2y-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\sqrt{2},-1),(\\sqrt{2},-1).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-2),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{2},-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-2)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},-1)$ and its minimum value is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483437 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f702bf3-bb64-5422-aea8-b0bbbff9042c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483437 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483444 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483444 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f621894-6c34-5b5d-bbbc-0b80620d0a44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483444 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186372202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483465 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483465 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "61c5b431-d83c-57cb-a714-7721ae5e576f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483465 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483477 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483477 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "76c5427c-e22a-57a5-a05e-2677ada8f489" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483477 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483508 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483508 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa39b7f6-ab97-5c13-a5d4-408471cf2b34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483508 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186281535 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
